#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134684} :activity/id #uuid "52c145b5-305d-5e94-a854-8ca571ca1a03" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135054 :tutorial/content "The **Gram-Schmidt process** is an algorithm that we can use to construct an *orthogonal* basis if we are given a non-orthogonal basis to start with.\n\nFor example, consider the basis $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ for the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n![](images/c2/c2543117bc661286a5965534dfeeaaf6a066281ee58e2f39ea35143971d6837f.svg)\n\nWe can use the above basis to construct an *orthogonal* basis for the given $2$-dimensional subspace. To do this, we use the Gram-Schmidt process, which involves $2$ steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Set ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}=\\mathbf{a}_1.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ in $V$ that's orthogonal to $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1.$ This can be achieved by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/67/678cc056e3c9d3fbf17423ac591b3e85d84ee8060c0637840fe1c4c9e87aa878.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1})$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So this means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\perp {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}.$\nMoreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nAfter applying the Gram-Schmidt process, we get the set $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}\\},$ which is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nSo, in our example, we start by setting ![](images/72/7221215504f7dc7a831501ee23c0563434dd414ac05c1ef7e5761c2719e7d24d.svg) Then, we find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ that's orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/59/5944c42ffd5e609dcec914718180be9652a817de6b8cf84439d57cb106f03353.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b833ab7-f6d4-5c93-8709-37ecf29bf023" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3631 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e23dc2ed-aace-586d-8f71-e7621bc6d386" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003"} :step/id #uuid "7dca9c20-2451-5bcc-8034-904527090719" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10"} :step/id #uuid "09154627-fce0-54d9-b9c1-232fc06abaf2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135055 :tutorial/content "Given a basis $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ for a subspace $V,$ we can use the Gram-Schmidt process to find an *orthonormal* basis of the subspace.\n\nTo do this, we simply execute the Gram-Schmidt process as before to obtain an orthogonal basis $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2\\},$ and then we normalize each vector in this basis to get the orthonormal basis $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1, \\mathbf{u}_2\\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_1}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_1 \\Vert},\n\t\\quad\n\t\\mathbf{u}_2 = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}_2}{\\Vert \\mathbf{v}_2 \\Vert}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ea8b19b-f7a8-5339-abd4-3069e18b2555" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3632 :tutorial/title "Using the Gram-Schmidt Process to Find an Orthonormal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "881045a6-4243-5fbd-8617-d6520ecad604" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731"} :step/id #uuid "5c0057df-8020-54b5-b1ba-1011db749edc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135089 :tutorial/content "Sometimes it's helpful to \"clean up\" the result of a Gram-Schmidt process by scaling the resulting vectors.\n\nFor example, suppose that the set $\\mathcal B,$ given by\n\n![](images/79/79d1a4963f0e8a2e47dda0acf5c6031571574b1631d57c15d757f94b4f25468b.svg)\n\nis an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}.$\n\nSince the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2,$ the vector $3\\mathbf{v}_2$ also lies in $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp 3\\mathbf{v}_2.$ Therefore, taking \n\n![](images/c6/c69d0c835aa55e7e835c81a4338d56bd3fda97ebb8a73ed370e4384b80d23093.svg)\n\ninstead of $\\mathbf{v}_2,$ we again obtain an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Span} \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/fc/fcdac688252b50d74ed8c4bddba578f027f3bfec5a356ec3fe54839638c1a0ae.svg)\n\nBut this time, the second vector in the basis looks slightly nicer and is probably easier to work with." :tutorial/id #uuid "00f219fa-5ae3-5e1d-acd0-7b7df752a433" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4351 :tutorial/title "Scaling the Vectors After the Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "8e33ff42-ffc9-57ae-941b-8a447aded2e4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134686}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134006} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414757} :activity/id #uuid "b568452d-b15b-5a64-b20e-4ac3b74285e9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414752 :question/id #uuid "9ea317f2-12b0-537c-bfd8-c2c151d6c85b"} :step/id #uuid "22215345-cf56-5cf6-8ec7-0d0ddc7f81fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414750 :question/id #uuid "0934e8ac-174d-5792-a7fc-4ab5bbca2aae"} :step/id #uuid "74c122ae-6f5f-55d9-980e-db9851e51f87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134311 :question/id #uuid "1ad7f9ba-9786-5d27-9e6a-080a7315cce4"} :step/id #uuid "84989845-fb61-500c-b47d-0a19bc3d185f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414753 :question/id #uuid "c7bb7823-c38b-5250-a2f7-3b306162dae1"} :step/id #uuid "99574453-db24-5adc-881e-60bd9dee9204" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414751 :question/id #uuid "44c51145-6075-534b-b54c-46371542f3eb"} :step/id #uuid "a30ac731-6001-5775-b224-b7080f289ca2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414756}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414719} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488314} :activity/id #uuid "b5c39511-7dca-5f46-9bd0-18a600b280c7" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272129 :question/id #uuid "3a029be5-b6a5-506a-8947-8d15614953ac"} :step/id #uuid "32bcfa5a-91f6-5b5e-bb38-21b8b56078c2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414750 :question/id #uuid "0934e8ac-174d-5792-a7fc-4ab5bbca2aae"} :step/id #uuid "4ef5a9e1-a6f9-549c-8de0-627265244a96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366525 :question/id #uuid "6dd16f7a-f327-54ce-81b8-5d78600e9324"} :step/id #uuid "515565f3-dbe0-5771-aba6-dfe3cfe75b5d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134312 :question/id #uuid "5fc90254-ed2d-508a-b704-d310ff8d6596"} :step/id #uuid "6d9cca53-b8e7-5866-80ca-e525fd9507fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351263 :question/id #uuid "46809f69-3556-5ede-b407-68133439141e"} :step/id #uuid "b145ad9e-f7c2-5436-b124-365beca417e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488306}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487560} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502208} :activity/id #uuid "c6f0a606-7357-5b62-ba3e-c7abaf986519" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414752 :question/id #uuid "9ea317f2-12b0-537c-bfd8-c2c151d6c85b"} :step/id #uuid "3587edc5-deb1-5629-b533-df76f3ade5d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502177}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354526 :question/id #uuid "dfa73306-0d2d-5aa8-89e7-e48bb1792f48"} :step/id #uuid "3e0438aa-82a5-54a7-85cc-d1569f51001a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502180 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326899 :question/id #uuid "4c9da90f-19cf-5646-82a3-18682358ed1b"} :step/id #uuid "560834e6-f0bc-5556-8407-8ecea32f604c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502192}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134337 :question/id #uuid "4388fdfa-5eb8-5165-a253-2f6080a33d4c"} :step/id #uuid "8acc1726-5d26-585c-9955-88497139998b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502175}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134310 :question/id #uuid "84b52cd1-ef6d-51a0-aeac-25f2437c64c6"} :step/id #uuid "f6bf6f1f-05a8-52ae-97c6-6d0881baed05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502180}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501459} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526151} :activity/id #uuid "2d8e4de7-07aa-5497-8520-580346c63c85" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134336 :question/id #uuid "5c8bae78-eaad-50ef-b636-171581008831"} :step/id #uuid "08124f83-7980-5571-b707-926cd78bf739" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272125 :question/id #uuid "915cd440-9e70-527f-ae9d-6fd020966a43"} :step/id #uuid "3898a6a3-4210-54de-9968-2837f9a2875f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326874 :question/id #uuid "2ffd5b6e-c9be-5714-a520-bbfc949845de"} :step/id #uuid "5b5b81b3-a78b-55c1-a4e4-7c40c4d6adf0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414753 :question/id #uuid "c7bb7823-c38b-5250-a2f7-3b306162dae1"} :step/id #uuid "eebd9c50-66ee-578e-a4cd-08abc3c0f2e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354527 :question/id #uuid "ab8ba96e-a444-5139-b50d-ae295e519e61"} :step/id #uuid "ff2416cc-3d95-5907-9a24-2d1ffac32e81" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526113}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process for Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525197}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133602 :question/id #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5581" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$\n\n![](images/23/23aedd2796ca55056409a6d0c32e0d5521283297398bd78d8c7ac3ce84c42fba.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/10/10e08ad8dd61b256018aa33488f94b563669fa91c739785488306d15160838c9.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/6e/6ec9d05c93c9ae7a3c3079d69b7c0b5d665fb295efaa918cea26f77988110342.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a9/a9fa0f03fc16c2d3f1c2102033c0ec6c7d4911ee2e1001a460893c85171204b8.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ac3559-dcd9-55f9-aac4-fcea3042c9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324409} {:answer/id #uuid "75b853f6-c16f-5e75-ab8b-d4faac5fb5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324949} {:answer/id #uuid "7697992d-74a5-5d9f-a0fa-6321a19e8150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324957} {:answer/id #uuid "9006fa7a-44e4-5a45-ab40-435538384d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325195} {:answer/id #uuid "95a62927-51a9-5f6e-86e7-9088ce3c3eb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325245} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad14920-f5c2-5677-8bae-927846f923fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326317}] :question/id #uuid "6cd93368-184b-51ef-8337-6d58bffaad14" :question/math-academy-id "q-40738" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e893c4-4997-5c8e-b8d7-c8971f6c56a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132056} {:answer/id #uuid "a96063c6-ce04-5df0-8bfe-418753e9924d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132057} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf645c0-1cf0-5a89-a9dd-efb31451fb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132058} {:answer/id #uuid "ca44e1b4-2447-5d68-b9a4-e6e7bc76fd52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132059} {:answer/id #uuid "66c3aea1-c4f1-551e-98db-cfe77d0ffb71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132060} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d510ee-e9f1-5ced-adea-9de4441c27e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132055}] :question/id #uuid "5c8bae78-eaad-50ef-b636-171581008831" :question/math-academy-id "q-41030" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34e399fc-1be9-55fc-9421-a3fb393c4a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324402} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9bcbaa-82e4-5ecb-975c-2de4686710b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324602} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b5efac-0f1c-57a5-8e12-d15d32e2c033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325934} {:answer/id #uuid "e91bf5c0-202e-5d41-8b25-1b262a4b04cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326025} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84a86c-a29e-5c3e-8c0c-dab2de42657c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325934} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d2da01-bae6-5a74-90e1-d728bf5bf7a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326583}] :question/id #uuid "fa171dfe-33fe-5987-b734-14774b114908" :question/math-academy-id "q-41032" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1334ae27-fd25-5463-9af2-ba4ec4924cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348613} {:answer/id #uuid "60c5e6e0-9b1d-5d73-9ad3-64b43fac8e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349329} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba27908-dcc4-538c-bef9-f7bbb79a9e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349439} {:answer/id #uuid "c180f877-9d4a-5814-8026-31cd971984d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350161} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d265a4-2275-5166-a086-e4b94003f59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350161} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc6a1ee-3c3c-508b-a728-6c227a57fe74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350727}] :question/id #uuid "9a9c1a7b-a005-5b4c-8323-017c8b7b9551" :question/math-academy-id "q-81209" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{4}{3},$ $c=\\frac{13}{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{13}{3})}=-\\frac{4}{13}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b088cb5-8750-5ec6-a5b3-88217ec4e0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352636} {:answer/id #uuid "8726e473-8cd5-5549-a888-1a32e7aa0565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352750} {:answer/id #uuid "9611d43b-9cdf-5a47-86aa-2d6bed6567ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352867} {:answer/id #uuid "e30eacef-dbd7-5b68-93b8-773a32a0917a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353541} {:answer/id #uuid "fb245c20-cec7-5c4d-ace6-99232601dbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352750} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4b402d1-0952-53bc-85cb-6e648bc7e4