r-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267949} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0413551-8e99-57e8-bb7f-e5638a7ca7b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268690}] :question/id #uuid "5999fc8e-7257-5b70-9229-12117f86cb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135529" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-1(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-6\\pm \\sqrt{(6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 13}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da76e31-175a-5286-8fd4-289cd1f97760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266265} {:answer/id #uuid "11f7b69f-6687-5032-9df4-3edb457b07c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266293} {:answer/id #uuid "19df29fb-9a7f-5158-96dc-263f0f5b9675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266357} {:answer/id #uuid "2af3b11f-beef-5eaa-81fa-1d192c81b682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266520} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3e5dd9-6830-5b8f-bed3-1b4efd1cbd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266951} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1fbf41-3329-5a41-b300-0f02dc01bdbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268737}] :question/id #uuid "62cbd463-845c-5e66-8284-1a8dd6e26538" :question/math-academy-id "q-135530" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )^{2}-4(-9)) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-144}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 12\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6acb102e-8d6a-525e-9de8-55099a7f7d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/ma