ollows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -13 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-2(-13)) & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 34}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32bb690a-1edd-598a-8f92-be2849b7ba3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561715} {:answer/id #uuid "433b5384-8a47-5bf4-8248-feeb545bb5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561745} {:answer/id #uuid "444d2ba3-d5e0-51cc-a7bf-2e59c0f7e046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561750} {:answer/id #uuid "c82b1b59-046b-5762-bdef-3f6575ddb33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562080} {:answer/id #uuid "cda8ecc1-0eed-5b98-9c26-6689db877867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561750} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f8092c-7c2b-5d58-ae67-c2f1ccabc279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562289}] :question/id #uuid "f2511236-69db-51c8-aebc-e7ad77137885" :question/math-academy-id "q-135531" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-7-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((-7-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +41) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{(10)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 41}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Block Diagonalization of a 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128949 :question/id #uuid "fb7c97e6-f106-5a47-ad01-75205c0d2603" :question/math-academy-id "e-5534" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n![](images/56/566569dbf0d6373b8482b41bc6fbc8b69d77eaf0cf5987151fe3854c77de9219.svg)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/7e/7e33a1ba83b16d1b5767ee773aaa5d797eaab72949e98bb51aed6fbd249ab386.svg)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/22/22f1869f42a464e5d682ae6e7b5b33c75b8a1d480869baa5fc036ffaaa4d8417.svg)\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} .$ Therefore, \n\n![](images/b1/b1f00ea903f35490283b270c21eb49e4934235a2173a0d3b25c8feac6a6a509d.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d159a3-8f7f-5702-9187-3f67068b60a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324013} {:answer/id #uuid "728999fd-5749-5cee-a90e-8647dd1c1b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324922} {:answer/id #uuid "a8842f3e-06fd-5dd8-aee7-73b4f4366e3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325406} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0c940a-9cc0-58ac-9e7a-d4fcacea51e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325427} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd2b8b8-b3db-5d04-82d4-deb11ee2260a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325406} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ec31f8-d7dd-5c84-9833-21e5dc79c7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326286}] :question/id #uuid "97813176-8f27-5690-98e4-6d6c62e9f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7da56c-ca2e-5a96-8857-4db9ccde332e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127598} {:answer/id #uuid "0b77ec29-7ced-562b-92f5-16f5eb37a6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127599} {:answer/id #uuid "34274851-b029-5ab6-9798-5073a2e55699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127600} {:answer/id #uuid "58362e0c-b125-5df4-9b84-a4e29f2fc2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127601} {:answer/id #uuid "65b7a354-76de-5231-b73f-82ad02a91c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -7 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f9bde63b-3d9d-5d49-ba03-d618c68268b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127597}] :question/id #uuid "5c2985f5-71df-5f93-9829-290000454e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38661" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} - 1 \\\\ -\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 + 4\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1$ and $λ_{2} =-2 + 3\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175ad214-847b-57ec-ba0a-35665534ee96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266337} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2d55e5-f79a-542a-ba87-67fb08e50bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266416} {:answer/id #uuid "48e6699e-ac33-5ea9-b694-64ef66b3dbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266796} {:answer/id #uuid "6adba04a-cab3-592c-9a8a-b48189435b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267090} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d056e9-1d8b-57ba-a405-6ee3b9e35e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268301} :answer-field/id #uuid "030bc847-8b2b-53be-9a9c-267a88e0fad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268408}] :question/id #uuid "2473b775-36eb-5e2f-9d42-ae0339f64aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d341ada-2ee2-588f-8ebf-372a6633f48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266396} {:answer/id #uuid "65086dbf-75b3-5a23-aaea-a82e58a68007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267038} {:answer/id #uuid "83504d57-835d-5eb9-a29b-d69f6cfc0eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267302} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d027a4-15ab-58b2-ad65-4ade490eeb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267792} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc1b892-6f10-5e8c-902e-29a37c0fb6a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71b045e-ec56-56dc-b521-a46b293afbbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268841}] :question/id #uuid "47b1e90f-9592-5fb0-9bb9-cad4410fafa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-135667" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-7\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2+7\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfca09c8-2fbf-5b61-a844-d6226f67969d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367424} {:answer/id #uuid "1a432586-67ea-579f-9def-1c43c3ec02fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367425} {:answer/id #uuid "16af2c3c-4d94-5eaa-986e-bbfe55a1e136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367426} {:answer/id #uuid "bc491017-15a3-583d-972c-b9a4c0bcf936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367427} {:answer/id #uuid "928cbd76-9987-5432-b8f9-b80432ea4e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367427} :answer-field/id #uuid "520555b5-5581-5d8f-89be-81935c065e6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367423}] :question/id #uuid "d2ced293-3358-55ba-a61e-e8eb5811597c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135649" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-5\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44dd643c-fe3b-5f48-9ca3-20ea66e7e0ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388425} {:answer/id #uuid "9691a744-a9fc-5fb6-8063-f3640da3bb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388426} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7e4a6b-3bc6-5a47-8484-de375f899e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388427} {:answer/id #uuid "d5075f70-7e15-5ae8-9d44-3360b54373c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388428} {:answer/id #uuid "7081f27a-51b4-5b4a-90b2-b391b8ebaecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388427} :answer-field/id #uuid "72e1a1ee-667f-52f2-b8b6-c16d81da25fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388424}] :question/id #uuid "f7453494-e18a-50f5-85b0-d29344213529" :question/math-academy-id "q-135641" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4+\\text{i} \\\\ 2+6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4-\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b219e25-b7de-54f7-986a-38c98e277a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388432} {:answer/id #uuid "d200c247-9d3b-5fa3-bd48-0ef09f7a0d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388433} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed58743-d23a-5a94-83d7-2860fff3387f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388434} {:answer/id #uuid "d59e6ebf-0843-5ef4-8fe1-d5fbc89f05aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388435} {:answer/id #uuid "829a0c6b-0865-5fd5-a46b-8e4ebd523c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388434} :answer-field/id #uuid "9afe0ea9-8480-5156-92b1-0524f8f503e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388431}] :question/id #uuid "8670ee42-0198-5e90-b231-dedde453ca87" :question/math-academy-id "q-135643" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+8\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5,$ 