x},\\,{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f74dc1-50df-595e-8161-b8f89331daa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400725} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7a423c-75c4-5e5a-b59c-84187ba85ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 2∓3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400726} {:answer/id #uuid "42d7d073-ef3b-52cf-9bc8-76d763c638be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400727} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ebde54-8171-5261-b9aa-5161a997d41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400728} {:answer/id #uuid "de8841f9-874d-500f-b2f5-42f3b26e4bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}∓4\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400727} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b4274e-d77f-5ccc-a441-ebbf054b029f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400724}] :question/id #uuid "b863886c-e733-5496-a6df-912807a8ccbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-135468" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1,2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317b66ea-a2ed-5b63-b2c7-67056caf8cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 8\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561712} {:answer/id #uuid "38d5a319-fe62-57c5-9d19-50dbddcdfa78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561724} {:answer/id #uuid "d197643c-d92d-5245-83d0-3b43e501d4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =7\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562107} {:answer/id #uuid "e8aff0e2-9d62-516e-8d28-e5fb7fafa6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 6\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562159} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd6bce9-f18f-53b1-9912-62a627ae6f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562214} :answer-field/id #uuid "009ce921-bb1e-52ba-8b94-ddf423054ad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562215}] :question/id #uuid "7a911604-8f85-54da-89bb-d261f841ad68" :question/math-academy-id "q-38917" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1,2}=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2019 :topic/title "Block Diagonalization of NxN Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130306} :activity/id #uuid "69de34ab-1f09-5179-b87a-f14938999f8e" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130402 :tutorial/content "How do we construct a particular rotation-scaling matrix $A?$\n\nFirst, recall that rotation matrices are of the form\n![](images/50/5027e188171ed6898ca18d22afc5fa4cadd1c9b95fa6d5f3ca738c1365884495.svg)\nHere, the matrix $R$ rotates a vector by an angle of $\\theta$ counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nScaling matrices are of the form\n![](images/b7/b73fad8b6f2fbd691d60b2b326ac5a1affd32dac1f2647a7096a44222defc817.svg)\nThe scaling matrix $S$ scales a vector by a scale factor of $r,$ where the origin is the center of enlargement. \n\nWe can write any rotation-scaling matrix $A$ as a product of a scaling matrix and a rotation matrix: \n\n$$\nA=SR\n$$\n\nOr, more explicitly:\n![](images/66/66f21dce4b71254563eea309f73587cc4e6d4a8b788a23e85425b31145ed191c.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a451bc3-5ec6-543a-911f-04a39b6fdcf4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4390 :tutorial/title "Constructing a Rotation-Scaling Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4206234b-6f29-5edd-8f26-20b644723a7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130322 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\big\\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation $\\mathbf A$ by its standard matrix $A,$ given by \n\n![](images/ac/ac86756a955ff34f5cd438373385537d40902ed764c24dcf70aebe0170509233.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $\\mathbf{A}$ by computing the matrix products as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=i+2\\,j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlotting the vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ and their images $\\mathbf i'$ and $\\mathbf j',$ we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/b5/b562af85ac2ad0f4871105d61b43aa8e27d215a4006c0ee3371cd673b2dd9579.png)\n\nThe transformation rotates the square by an angle of \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}1}\\right) \\approx 63.4^\\circ,\n$$\n\nthen scales it by a scale factor of \n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{{\\color{blue}1}^2 + {\\color{red}2}^2} = \\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nIn fact, any matrix of the form \n![](images/de/de48c49adcb549b17f02f1f40d04d7817b6e5bd255ee390317be8da0a0b9bb3c.svg)\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ and $\\color{red}b$ are real numbers (not both equal to zero simultaneously) is a matrix that rotates and then scales. \nThese types of matrices are called **rotation-scaling matrices**." :tutorial/id #uuid "81772221-4f86-554a-a3a0-b51dc4c6d12e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2012 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4570acd4-fcc3-52d3-a60f-f7dacdc74dad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359bc41e-1b6d-5177-9642-4f8697bc5ba0"} :step/id #uuid "fd76e3d2-8f30-54e7-b718-5d23d3d0401f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130323 :tutorial/content "We've seen that we can write a rotation-scaling matrix $A$ is the product of a rotation and a scaling matrix:\n\n![](images/2f/2f8ddb3f34a2e05e7d0d222dbe693328d7a9d8e7563c9fb44933231fdc453aa5.svg)\n\nSo, the natural question now is, how does a rotation-scaling matrix \n![](images/77/77cb6dbe3ef4dd36908e6f4a93ac5133c25d81af4502f3a396e2b0e4f9716fc8.svg)\nrelate to the individual rotation and scaling matrices?\n\nFirst, setting the two equal to each other, we get\n\n![](images/cd/cd6202c0ab3e908d20fa1ceca61f3a7d1baceaa7187a64e7c2a7030b0fa8f0e2.svg)\n\nFrom this, we can find the scale factor and angle of rotation of a rotation-scaling matrix. First, notice that we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo find $r$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we can square and then add the two equations above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+{b}^{2} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the scale factor $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nr = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2} = \\sqrt{\\det{A}}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the rotation angle $\\theta$ in terms of $a$ and $b,$ we start with the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}r\\cos\\theta}, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}b} = {\\color{red}r\\sin\\theta}.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{b}{a}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac ba \\right).\n$$\n\nIt's also possible to recover both the original scaling *and* rotation matrix once the scale factor $r$ is known. Let's see how that works." :tutorial/id #uuid "1fb2c1d9-0856-55ca-945a-8e01f2e58902" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2014 :tutorial/title "Finding the Scale Factor and Rotation Angle Of a Rotation-Scaling Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e43d8235-fa21-5652-b196-42d8e706cde2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5304084-5020-5bac-afe0-a4eaf6f0272d"} :step/id #uuid "1cd1b6e2-db31-5859-8ab0-4539c67bdc7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c15923f7-ddc0-5012-9645-922ef36cce97"} :step/id #uuid "25e203cd-b5fa-517f-8b98-1bad7223ac86"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71ea2c0c-8bcb-5674-a938-4357302d6429"} :step/id #uuid "8615c849-580e-5043-ba3f-ab9b5733b43c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130308}}] :activity/title "Rotation-Scaling Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129360}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050603 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129321 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128885 :question/id #uuid "c9af0fcb-c124-58f9-b8df-76db9b1c1520" :question/math-academy-id "e-2174" :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. ![](images/b9/b9afd3e5f09e1e09796dd63c7361c3e651a84e3593cc89d047ba2e04a6a5a497.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/f9/f970cb378245ca70c4ecfbee257eb96ff8e596bc60d49f39902de5097634ff05.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/94/948eeaaca61b3847db56ecf55a86330b44f5fe0cd263b3a14596f2f2d8c5ebaf.svg)" :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form ![](images/3a/3a0ad5f97ad286573cb6a9602450fd4cadbdfd1e9f712e67ecb749422a99fd2b.svg) where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- ![](images/44/4463a1d0d745ded4e14578618190f63963cbfa2daa06cb7553120bcfb431f674.svg) is a rotation-scaling matrix (with $a=7$ and $b=1$).\n\n- ![](images/8d/8d38fa886ca5a74fc14a26b4a39c503b7abecb90f0b3c3eb83f93dab950bdd20.svg) is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- ![](images/8b/8bab782c2e7e2ce43a3d30a9f3ae669df354c8fb802baef5f96d9b2791efdd0e.svg) is a rotation-scaling matrix (with $a=-8$ and $b=-3$).\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "359bc41e-1b6d-5177-9642-4f8697bc5ba0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "562f2805-9f09-5e89-b09c-94c4809435a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459909} {:answer/id #uuid "feaa5d1c-ea0d-51b8-a656-0e78ecb7f2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459910} :answer-field/id #uuid "743ed65d-70d0-5027-9c43-b6b316cce648" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460821} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b08d56f9-5c6e-5427-8ac8-973f2cb2c0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459911} {:answer/id #uuid "f842c9c8-bda5-5681-b9c2-ac48835e9445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459911} :answer-field/id #uuid "21401d46-cadc-5ebc-9505-4a1961e95c21" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "535db015-38fb-51e0-b841-97ef9b67c6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459913} {:answer/id #uuid "bac13582-2752-58d1-a164-33571b4f5329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459914} :answer-field/id #uuid "681d3363-4bb9-5097-bd6c-7727036faa45" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460823}] :question/id #uuid "d3837446-5faf-5668-beb3-7a2ca40df6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-38517" :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **III.** $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c772b547-2490-5a6b-b1c7-805dbf72ff92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127442} {:answer/id #uuid "edbddb41-b604-55c1-9006-b66e675d21f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127443} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb31310-2aac-5326-bb38-47b84c157569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127444} {:answer/id #uuid "25d2d54d-f013-59ee-909e-fae62c7bfbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127445} {:answer/id #uuid "6de69e9a-5a3b-5952-b096-70c072f3b84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127444} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12caa1b-7b3c-5396-95e8-4140e83e7317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127441}] :question/id #uuid "d2763490-08ca-5ea9-a7a6-a16fa5f7a313" :question/math-academy-id "q-38526" :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aebd1d9b-7682-54a1-87d1-5625b9a74a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467440} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1c3ef4-6d14-5347-a96d-aa3d56907e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467441} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c341505-ef51-58ef-9929-830b95c5da1b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type